
Player: This Atomon looks different from the others.

Player: It seems to have a complete arrangement of electrons around its nucleus.

Player: Dr. Mundo mentioned that the electrons in the outermost energy level are called valence electrons.

Player: I wonder how many valence electrons Neon has.

Player: What is the electron arrangement of Neon?

- 2,8
  Player: Correct! Neon has two electrons in its first energy level and eight in its second energy level.

- 2,7
  Player: Not quite. Neon has ten electrons in total. Its electron arrangement is 2,8.

- 2,8,1
  Player: Not quite. That arrangement belongs to Sodium. Neon has an electron arrangement of 2,8.

Player: So Neon has eight valence electrons!

Player: Its outermost energy level is complete.

Player: That explains why Neon is generally very unreactive compared with many other elements.

Player: Neon is an example of an atom that already has a stable outer electron arrangement.

[signal arg="quest_notify:quest_chain_002_01:observe_neon"]

Player: I should remember this observation when I compare Neon with the other Atomons.
